#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188004 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188297 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1bff423b-022b-5914-a134-c4c346e91b8f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Horizontal Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "70e3ea0e-2f39-5715-98a3-2a0e686ffa76" :question/math-academy-id "e-10116" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=2(1+\\sin\\theta)$ for $\\theta \\in \\left[0, 2\\pi\\right).$ Given that \n\n$$\nx'(\\theta) =  2\\cos2\\theta - 2\\sin\\theta, \\qquad\ny'(\\theta) = 2\\cos\\theta+2\\sin2\\theta,\n$$\n\nand $x'\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = 0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y'\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}2\\right) = 0$\n\nII. $\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi/2} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.$\n\n*Hint: Use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point $\\theta_0$, there is a horizontal tangent at $\\theta_0$ if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta_0)}{x'(\\theta_0)} = 0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta_0) = 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x'(\\theta_0) \\neq 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at $\\theta_0$ yet the derivative $y'(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that $x'\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}2\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac{3\\pi}2$ into the expression for $y'(\\theta),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi ) \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta\\to \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}(\\frac{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2})}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2\\cdot (-1)}{-2\\cdot 0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/1b/1bff05165761ba775dfae73cd4d41c28f5a939b8caca602d516636a6fb05ae75.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note**: We'll discuss vertical tangents in more detail later in this lesson."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188023 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25fd0bb9-4a47-5f76-bdba-cdcd19cf141f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Vertical Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "2175ea86-60e7-5402-810d-1372b455c28d" :question/math-academy-id "e-10244" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=\\cot^2\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in \\left(0, \\pi\\right).$ Given that \n\n$$\nx'(\\theta) = -\\cot\\theta(\\cos\\theta + 2\\cot\\theta\\csc\\theta), \\qquad\ny'(\\theta) = -\\cot\\theta\\csc\\theta(1+\\sin^2\\theta),\n$$\n\nand $y'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $x'\\left(\\dfrac\\pi2\\right) = 0$\n\nII. $\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}=-\\infty$\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\to {0}^{+}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{-} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\to {0}^{-}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{+}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta = \\theta_0$ if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta_0)}{x'(\\theta_0)}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta_0) \\neq 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x'(\\theta_0) = 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at $\\theta_0$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0^{-}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that $y'\\left(\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi2$ into the expression for $x'(\\theta),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (0+2\\cdot 0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta\\to\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^+,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta (1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{-\\cancel{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta (1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-\\cancel{cot}\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta (1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the second hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part I, and the first hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ \n\n![](images/2d/2dc0665a0f979fd247d010a7b6d5891a758eff7e5357b881011ddf0d3f2f2002.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188297 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1bff423b-022b-5914-a134-c4c346e91b8f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Horizontal Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1a30c16-211c-5885-bd73-60eba0b26337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185677} {:answer/id #uuid "eebe674e-a895-53a5-8284-bb2fe739487c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185678} {:answer/id #uuid "00554a1e-d43f-5aca-89a9-07841ea6af07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185679} {:answer/id #uuid "3472b7ea-9bc6-58cd-bf1a-0790d8d48e2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185680} {:answer/id #uuid "6db8d629-42cc-5562-a4b8-39f1b9893e3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00554a1e-d43f-5aca-89a9-07841ea6af07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185679} :answer-field/id #uuid "320ef1c8-e7ec-58a4-b0b7-68dce67c7b5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46af20ff-a82f-5003-946a-59bcece07f0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143555" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\pi /2$\n\n*Hint: Use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0-0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-2\\cdot (-1)}{0+2\\cdot 0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+2}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/2c/2c1b42bf3a8c1b5f8755e02a162e1554904bbc432285cb8ba8c7b06181e1cc03.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188297 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1bff423b-022b-5914-a134-c4c346e91b8f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Horizontal Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32345ee7-7a47-5474-a2bb-6e4a4aea50e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548455} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5190c4-8756-52a6-96ba-4837d6e37505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548561} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ce0a40-fa35-5f06-883c-f11fe791d936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548688} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ae8e98-0ca4-56d8-aaa8-7d7045c480d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548750} {:answer/id #uuid "f9120367-b038-51b1-82ec-64924db5b41f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9120367-b038-51b1-82ec-64924db5b41f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548852} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae25116c-2863-575b-8604-36e46667778c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "613239a3-89a3-5820-a45d-8b5d5da600ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-143586" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3$ for $\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0$ at $\\theta =0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0$\n\n*Hint: Use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =0$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta ))^{′}}{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{-3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, there is a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0.$\n\n![](images/9e/9ef9840644e37fbb660ede7d574d8814b9ce7ce3bd0ff3790b5c5d21420f3e70.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25fd0bb9-4a47-5f76-bdba-cdcd19cf141f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Vertical Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6cd7e58-adc6-5d97-83dc-0ef80f38597f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185683} {:answer/id #uuid "1fe0e570-a22a-5641-93a8-f6671eba3fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185684} {:answer/id #uuid "2b2627b4-92aa-5fc7-b855-d9ad614c65ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185685} {:answer/id #uuid "37a2400b-425f-50cd-9cf2-4636a9355424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185686} {:answer/id #uuid "61adcb0d-059f-5544-a8ef-924174a4c953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fe0e570-a22a-5641-93a8-f6671eba3fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185684} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6707d96-ec88-5b35-acfc-f90c52c72973" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9cda14ea-b780-59bc-8a48-d618ff7d920f" :question/math-academy-id "q-143587" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1$ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\to {0}^{-}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{-} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\to {0}^{+}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{+}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{-}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1+2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the first hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part II, and the second hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/51/515b4cbcb39ad80d4401adffd450aed9e222663c469f1cf1cf36cbd900b5eb4b.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188297 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1bff423b-022b-5914-a134-c4c346e91b8f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Horizontal Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7edb7d92-e818-544e-823b-3f4730b7ef09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548596} {:answer/id #uuid "89060f9d-efdf-54d3-92e8-0dd1496bbd98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548618} {:answer/id #uuid "97933b81-d675-57e7-930c-b4fb844e708e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548651} {:answer/id #uuid "b433e913-f866-5b33-9b59-0a4885da5fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548716} {:answer/id #uuid "e3777b0b-88ad-5005-9923-c4af38304f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b433e913-f866-5b33-9b59-0a4885da5fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548716} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2b0c7cd-ed3d-59d4-a4a4-9ad3a4bcbf7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f78e73c-c303-57f1-975d-3a308d3af577" :question/math-academy-id "q-143690" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0$ at $\\theta =\\pi ,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\pi $\n\n*Hint: Use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\pi )=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\pi $ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\pi ) \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\pi )}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the limit in part II equals $0,$ there is a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0.$\n\n![](images/d1/d1308067e18807f159aee95433ea645be71abd153aa03888ef8092cf6fe226bd.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188297 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1bff423b-022b-5914-a134-c4c346e91b8f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Horizontal Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d5d7f63-4773-5ead-8708-dd1a010b746b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548410} {:answer/id #uuid "32901a19-e02f-575c-8024-84512c37ec77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548456} {:answer/id #uuid "e1125bf9-4c3c-53b1-989c-df79c04e9962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548791} {:answer/id #uuid "f0275ae7-961c-5830-84f1-365d4ad8c89e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548833} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d2719f-efb2-5cdd-a3fc-2255411e9071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1125bf9-4c3c-53b1-989c-df79c04e9962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548791} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bc92c9a-d12a-524b-92a8-ef263ab3cb9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85bc8151-a6d7-5a1f-86a0-0e52ad8f1c73" :question/math-academy-id "q-143691" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0$ at $\\theta =0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =0$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{{sin}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{2\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)(3{cos}^{2}⁡(0)-{sin}^{2}⁡(0))}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (3\\cdot 1-0)}{2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the limit in part II equals $0,$ there curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0.$\n\n![](images/a4/a48593a3bbcc61afaf80de619be505cb6dac58c559e03a0eab4553bd5a1e5013.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188297 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1bff423b-022b-5914-a134-c4c346e91b8f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Horizontal Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c9393e8-ba50-577d-bc72-427a22d73c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548442} {:answer/id #uuid "643536bd-f153-53c3-950f-0557b9212659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548546} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd84e4c-427d-5399-8b03-e39a998a285a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548735} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ecdef1-5b9f-50e3-ad8c-ca6553b623db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548751} {:answer/id #uuid "d784e426-65aa-55a1-bf4a-3e0846cd4ebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c9393e8-ba50-577d-bc72-427a22d73c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548442} :answer-field/id #uuid "20ad0273-0b41-526f-89b9-432d7d34c4cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "464ccc1b-341e-50cc-a320-81b9eb3e2aa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-143720" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{\\cancel{3}}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/8b/8b68110c25bfa464c0971a396ec50908c0c0622cbdc92bf42239b984695655ca.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25fd0bb9-4a47-5f76-bdba-cdcd19cf141f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Vertical Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ba9a9c7-f876-5d21-ad58-1993aa10b700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548585} {:answer/id #uuid "b823e1fd-ed55-5af9-8476-217b9d2d2816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548721} {:answer/id #uuid "d0a30101-e4dc-5b47-b643-52f9a3f0157a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548764} {:answer/id #uuid "d2799d84-2613-5167-82f5-661dd78222f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548767} {:answer/id #uuid "e3855878-ba32-5feb-81b3-dfb3a2d666f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0a30101-e4dc-5b47-b643-52f9a3f0157a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548764} :answer-field/id #uuid "d36c8c24-f03d-546f-b34a-dde62ed00658" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9323ccd3-7220-5dbc-9cd7-954d1072bf99" :question/math-academy-id "q-144998" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\to {0}^{-}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{-} \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\to {0}^{+}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{+}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-1-(-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{-}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ))}^{′}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi ) \\\\  & =-(-1)+2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the first hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part II, and the second hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/f4/f4462c7c41e7d6d5d10acec7627ac54f73477569356e22857659f5110847302d.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25fd0bb9-4a47-5f76-bdba-cdcd19cf141f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Vertical Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "075ae8f3-57cb-5b60-ba7d-7b522771c11d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548370} {:answer/id #uuid "1b055083-25b0-50bd-915e-94df9cc030f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548400} {:answer/id #uuid "321c8f80-1da5-598f-8ac9-48d513b22952" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548454} {:answer/id #uuid "a9848c7d-6940-59ef-bdc8-e27fe80c778e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548689} {:answer/id #uuid "b874085c-4187-5a6d-8924-748d9c4f64b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "075ae8f3-57cb-5b60-ba7d-7b522771c11d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548370} :answer-field/id #uuid "810830be-38ed-57ee-8e7a-4d8bf93a0313" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0404d67e-b2ba-59db-a275-bcabad92e41d" :question/math-academy-id "q-145010" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )\\to {0}^{-}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{-} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )\\to {0}^{+}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{+}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{-}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))^{′}}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))^{′}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi ) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the first hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2\\cdot {0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part II, and the second hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2\\cdot {0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/f9/f9a4fc8fa61dac84d3dbc4bc48f86e2b702f7854b1fd1d3b3e5f4303fe7769a7.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25fd0bb9-4a47-5f76-bdba-cdcd19cf141f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Vertical Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aaf653d-0c2b-54b8-b819-d44c0fbe6880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548378} {:answer/id #uuid "1c8ad316-23d2-5788-a2fe-5f1b87f5e5c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548404} {:answer/id #uuid "2cc6d1eb-c42d-5931-9dbe-d36709d00384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548443} {:answer/id #uuid "7a0f8ef7-8816-5c3a-91f4-4b087e45c987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548581} {:answer/id #uuid "8e29dbac-2fa1-558b-8390-191d5f378a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a0f8ef7-8816-5c3a-91f4-4b087e45c987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548581} :answer-field/id #uuid "52fae6cf-133f-598f-bd90-ed8251199a12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8abdd58d-cab9-5ebc-bd19-2d063796fe12" :question/math-academy-id "q-145211" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=8{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta -8{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=16{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1),\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0$" :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =0$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {0}^{-},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{16{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{16{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{16{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{16{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{24{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{16{sin}^{\\cancel{3}}⁡\\theta \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{24\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{cos}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)(3{cos}^{2}⁡(0)-1)}{24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{24} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the right limit of our derivative is not infinite, the curve does *not* have a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0.$ In fact, it is a horizontal tangent.\n\n![](images/4a/4a525b9a7a04f228bb45dc8c0c1cb916d1e98d8907e5fb1411ea52eb964c96ef.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25fd0bb9-4a47-5f76-bdba-cdcd19cf141f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Vertical Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e4d7f5-507c-5de7-88d4-ea15a0e80797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548360} {:answer/id #uuid "20d01cf0-d95f-5916-b4f3-763c5a6e9849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548416} {:answer/id #uuid "75b52c4a-dd39-5248-b7ac-60a9f8d0e11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548570} {:answer/id #uuid "7da7cab9-5764-5643-bc50-07cd0bd2f864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548591} {:answer/id #uuid "af09275f-fbfc-52f1-b658-e8836b59b4f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7da7cab9-5764-5643-bc50-07cd0bd2f864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548591} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec36f9e6-099c-5944-9227-f178df8ff3ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0e768435-2535-570e-8108-8e3e9452ca28" :question/math-academy-id "q-145215" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2),\\,\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0$" :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =0$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & ={tan}^{2}⁡(0)\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)({cos}^{2}⁡(0)+2) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1\\cdot ({1}^{2}+2) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {0}^{+},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2)}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{{tan}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2)}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)({cos}^{2}⁡(0)+2)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 1\\cdot ({1}^{2}+2)}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the right limit of our derivative is not infinite, the curve does *not* have a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0.$ In fact, it is a horizontal tangent.\n\n![](images/49/498b937eba18870a36ee6f09bb99cf7dde38eb95c4cc2aa6edcb46080d0fcf04.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
